<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chippenham</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Chippenham is a market town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wiltshire">Wiltshire</a>, south-west <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bristol">Bristol</a> Avon. It was a royal estate of the West <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> kings, and in January 878 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danes">Danes</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guthrum">Guthrum</a> seized it in a surprise winter attack that drove <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfred-the-great">Alfred the Great</a> into the marshes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athelney">Athelney</a>. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-edington">Battle of Edington</a> later that year, the Danes surrendered to Alfred at their stronghold, probably Chippenham.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chippenham.jpg" alt="Chippenham" width="320" height="302" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: My another account · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:St_Andrew%27s_Chippenham.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Chippenham lies in north-west <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wiltshire">Wiltshire</a>, 13 miles north-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bath">Bath</a> and 86 miles west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, at a prominent crossing of the River Avon. It is set between the Marlborough Downs to the east, the southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotswolds">Cotswolds</a> to the north and west, and Salisbury Plain to the south-east. Some form of settlement is believed to have existed there since before Roman times, and remains of Romano-British settlements are visible in a wall behind the former magistrates' court.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxon-chronicle">Anglo-Saxon Chronicle</a> records the town as Cippanhamme. The name may refer to a person called Cippa who had his hamm, an enclosure in a river meadow, or it may come from the Old English word ceap, meaning market. The name is recorded as Cippanhamm in 878, Cepen in 1042, Cheppeham in 1155 and Chippenham in 1227. The town was a royal vill, probably a royal hunting lodge, under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfred-the-great">Alfred the Great</a>. In Norman times the royal properties were divided into the manors of Sheldon, Rowden and Lowden, and the town expanded into Langstret, now the Causeway, from 1245 and into Le Newstret from 1406.</p><p>The A4 through the town incorporates parts of a 14th-century road linking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bristol">Bristol</a>, which was important for the cloth trade and was maintained partly with money from Bristol cloth merchants. The wool industry took off in the 16th century, partly because of the river. Plague struck in 1611 and 1636, and a recession in woollen cloth and poor corn harvests in 1622 and 1623 caused hardship. In 1747 a bribery scandal involving two of the town's members of parliament contributed to the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-walpole">Sir Robert Walpole</a>'s government.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>A branch of the Wilts and Berks Canal reached Chippenham in 1798, ending at a wharf near the market place, and the Great Western Railway arrived in 1841, attracting new businesses and bringing house building north of the line. The railway station has arches and other buildings engineered by Isambard Kingdom Brunel. The original Buttercross, of about 1570, was moved in 1889 to Castle Combe and re-erected in the town in 1995. Markets are held in the town centre each Friday and Saturday, and the museum in the Market Place tells the story of the town from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> times. At the 2021 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> the parish had 36,548 inhabitants.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Chippenham” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In January 878 a surprise Danish attack on <b>Chippenham</b> drove Alfred into the marshes of Athelney, but in May he defeated Guthrum at the Battle of Edington.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-wessex">Kingdom of Wessex · Military</a></li></ul></section>
